for the fact that you're right?

229
00:12:32,933 --> 00:12:35,233
-Give me an example, smarty-pants.
-All right. Okay.

230
00:12:35,333 --> 00:12:38,099
Give me an example
where I'm right and you're wrong,

231
00:12:38,199 --> 00:12:40,500
and you need to be shown
that you're wrong.

232
00:12:40,566 --> 00:12:42,666
And, by the way, take your time.

233
00:12:43,266 --> 00:12:46,733
[gentle music playing]

234
00:12:51,599 --> 00:12:54,400
So, next week, I'm gonna go...

235
00:12:55,766 --> 00:12:57,233
Why's that funny?

236
00:12:57,433 --> 00:12:59,066
You're acting like
we're in the kitchen, eating.

237
00:12:59,133 --> 00:13:01,566
Because when you're not sure
how it's gonna be taken,

238
00:13:01,633 --> 00:13:03,500
you look down, you have to busy yourself.

239
00:13:03,566 --> 00:13:06,266
So, next week, me and some girlfriends

240
00:13:06,433 --> 00:13:09,166
-are gonna go to a spa.
-That sounds nice.

241
00:13:09,233 --> 00:13:13,166
But I'm gonna need you
to be all over the kids.

242
00:13:13,266 --> 00:13:16,966
Lenny has cello, Persephone has ballet,

243
00:13:17,033 --> 00:13:18,466
and Cassiopeia...

244
00:13:18,566 --> 00:13:20,433
Were you looking
in a telescope last night?

245
00:13:21,633 --> 00:13:23,333
But you're too happy about that.

246
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:25,533
-You're never...
-You're going to the spa...

247
00:13:25,599 --> 00:13:27,966
-You're not giving me any conflict.
-Why should I?

248
00:13:28,099 --> 00:13:30,866
-You don't deserve any.
-I need something to get me...

249
00:13:30,933 --> 00:13:33,000
Well, then come up with a better premise.

250
00:13:33,199 --> 00:13:34,233
All right.

251
00:13:34,914 --> 00:13:35,815
I've met someone...

252
00:13:35,881 --> 00:13:38,417
[both laugh]

253
00:13:48,666 --> 00:13:50,466
-[Jerry] Thank you.
-[Ali] Thank you.

254
00:13:52,099 --> 00:13:54,933
[Ali] Now, when you take these cars out,
is there an option to buy?

255
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,837
-Are these cars for sale?
-Everything's for sale, honey.

256
00:13:58,333 --> 00:14:00,599
-Even you.
-That I know.

257
00:14:00,666 --> 00:14:02,099
-Yeah.
-That I know.

258
00:14:03,099 --> 00:14:05,733
Do you know
there was once a well-known writer

259
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:08,099
who offered me $40,000.

260
00:14:08,233 --> 00:14:11,233
I would keep that in my back pocket
in your next fight with George.

261
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:14,366
-"I'll have you know..."
-"Forty." "Forty?"

262
00:14:15,033 --> 00:14:17,066
-It's an odd number, by the way.
-Yeah.

263
00:14:17,133 --> 00:14:19,300
Fifty, I could see. Twenty, I could see.

264
00:14:19,366 --> 00:14:21,533
Forty is like
he thought about it a little.

265
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,566
"She's worth 50. Maybe I can get 10 off."

266
00:14:28,333 --> 00:14:31,033
Here's what I don't understand.
The guys, that years later,

267
00:14:31,099 --> 00:14:34,199
you find out that he had a whole family
in a different state.

268
00:14:34,266 --> 00:14:36,900
-Charles Lindbergh and Charles Kuralt.
-Exhausting.

269
00:14:36,966 --> 00:14:39,400
And who are the wives
that haven't figured that out?

270
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:41,500
Come on. Women are like pigs to truffles.

271
00:14:41,566 --> 00:14:43,866
Where were you that three-day weekend?
We didn't see you.

272
00:14:43,933 --> 00:14:45,699
You didn't call, you didn't...

273
00:14:45,933 --> 00:14:48,699
[Jerry] I pitched my wife Mormonism.

274
00:14:48,766 --> 00:14:50,599
Here's the genius of polygamy.

275
00:14:50,866 --> 00:14:55,000
Most women do not want
a man around that much.

276
00:14:55,066 --> 00:14:57,833
-Yeah.
-Once a week, twice a week.

277
00:14:57,900 --> 00:14:58,966
-Plenty.
-Yeah.

278
00:14:59,233 --> 00:15:03,166
Most men would like to be
with different women.

279
00:15:03,233 --> 00:15:05,366
-Yeah.
-If you can afford it,

280
00:15:05,599 --> 00:15:06,766
where's the victim?

281
00:15:08,733 --> 00:15:10,733
[Ali] Well, in Mormonism,
they all live together.

282
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,800
-[Jerry] Yeah.
-That's too many people for me.

283
00:15:12,866 --> 00:15:15,966
-Right, I agree with that.
-I don't... No, that's too much.

284
00:15:16,066 --> 00:15:19,266
I think the real loser
in that scenario is the man.

285
00:15:19,333 --> 00:15:22,099
Because men think they want more women.

286
00:15:22,166 --> 00:15:25,000
You don't want more women.
One woman is plenty.

287
00:15:28,533 --> 00:15:32,599
[Ali] We're at that stage where the kids
are happily ensconced in school.

288
00:15:32,666 --> 00:15:36,866
We're not gonna do anything wild.
We're not adopting a Korean boy.

289
00:15:36,933 --> 00:15:39,733
-We're not gonna mix it up too much.
-Right.

290
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:41,866
The little Korean boy,
right now watching this,

291
00:15:41,933 --> 00:15:43,266
a tear just rolled down his face.

292
00:15:43,333 --> 00:15:44,433
Right.

293
00:15:44,966 --> 00:15:47,533
[Jerry] I remember George telling me
one of the greatest husband lines

294
00:15:47,599 --> 00:15:49,466
I've ever heard,
and I'm gonna do it for you now.

295
00:15:49,533 --> 00:15:50,533
Okay.

296
00:15:50,599 --> 00:15:53,733
[Jerry] We were talking about how
you guys moved up here from D.C.,

297
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:56,233
and you're living
in a New York apartment now.

298
00:15:56,300 --> 00:15:57,933
Are you happy here?

299
00:15:58,166 --> 00:16:00,599
He goes, "Geez, I sure hope we are."

300
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:03,662
[both laugh]

301
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:05,566
[Jerry] The point being, he has no idea.

302
00:16:05,633 --> 00:16:08,066
-He'll be informed if he's happy.
-[Ali] Yes, exactly. Yes.

303
00:16:12,866 --> 00:16:14,433
[Ali] Do you know, when I first met you,

304
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:17,533
you were trying to remember the joke
about the lemon meringue pie.

305
00:16:17,766 --> 00:16:20,133
-[Jerry] No, it's Bavarian cream pie.
-That's what it was!

306
00:16:20,199 --> 00:16:21,500
-[Jerry] Bavarian cream pie.
-Yep.

307
00:16:21,566 --> 00:16:24,933
Here's the joke. So, there's a man.
He's a soldier in World War II.

308
00:16:25,166 --> 00:16:30,633
He stops into a little German bakery,
has a piece of Bavarian cream pie.

309
00:16:30,699 --> 00:16:32,333
It's the greatest thing he's ever had.

310
00:16:32,766 --> 00:16:36,666
The war ends, he goes home,
he gets married, raises a family,

311
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:40,566
the kids go to college,
the wife eventually passes away,

312
00:16:40,633 --> 00:16:45,099
he goes to the doctor, the doctor says,
"You have six months to live."

313
00:16:45,166 --> 00:16:47,233
He's done everything
he wanted to do in life.

314
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:51,866
There's only one thing
that he would like to experience again,

315
00:16:51,933 --> 00:16:56,599
and that is to have another piece
of that Bavarian cream pie.

316
00:16:56,800 --> 00:16:59,433
And so, he books passage
on an ocean liner.

317
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:02,266
Halfway across the Atlantic,
the ocean liner hits an iceberg and sinks.

318
00:17:02,333 --> 00:17:05,099
The life raft runs out of air
and he falls into the water.

319
00:17:05,166 --> 00:17:08,133
Now he starts to swim,
but he's getting eaten by sharks.

320
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:11,800
His legs and arms are both completely
eaten off and he's lost a lot of blood.

321
00:17:11,866 --> 00:17:15,033
But he lands on the coast of France,
and he begins to crawl.

322
00:17:16,466 --> 00:17:19,266
He crawls across France,

323
00:17:20,133 --> 00:17:21,433
across Switzerland,

324
00:17:21,766 --> 00:17:24,266
finds his way into Germany,
asking directions.

325
00:17:24,399 --> 00:17:28,066
He crawls into the bakery,

326
00:17:28,166 --> 00:17:32,133
pulls himself onto an empty stool.
No arms, no legs,

327
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:34,566
cancer riddled through his body and brain.

328
00:17:34,633 --> 00:17:38,066
The waitress says,
"May I take your order?"

329
00:17:38,666 --> 00:17:42,233
He says, "I would like a piece
of Bavarian cream pie."

330
00:17:42,466 --> 00:17:47,300
The waitress says, "Oh, gee.
I'm sorry. I just sold our last piece."

331
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:49,833
-Yeah?
-He goes, "I'll have peach."

332
00:17:51,500 --> 00:17:54,333
[Ali] I think it's funnier if she said,
"I just sold the last piece."

333
00:17:54,373 --> 00:17:56,375
Then he should go,
"I'll just have a cup of coffee."

334
00:17:56,442 --> 00:17:57,810
[laughing]

335
00:17:57,899 --> 00:17:59,433
I think it's better!

336
00:17:59,833 --> 00:18:01,399
-It is. You're right.
-Yeah!

